+### 2.2a The Boundary Encoding Problem (operational)
 
-Here's the concrete source of the $O(n \log n)$ term. Suppose the optimal protocol for $f$ is a binary tree of depth at most $\text{IC}_K(f)$ (or more carefully, has average transcript length at most $\text{IC}_K(f)$ under the worst-case input). To delimit $n$ variable-length transcripts, you need approximately $\log n$ bits per transcript — enough to specify where each one starts. That gives you $O(n \log n)$ total overhead.
+Here is the **first** concrete source of overhead. To solve $f^n$, the naive protocol concatenates $n$ independent sub-transcripts: $M_1 M_2 \cdots M_n$. But a concatenated string is not self-delimiting. To reconstruct each individual sub-transcript, the receiver must know where each one begins and ends.
 
-More formally: given the concatenated transcript $M_1 M_2 \cdots M_n$, Bob (knowing his $n$ inputs $y_1, \ldots, y_n$ and the protocol $\pi$) can simulate the protocol *forward*. But to extract the individual contributions $K(M_i \mid y_i, \pi)$ and sum them, he needs to know where one transcript ends and the next begins. Without a prefix-free encoding, he needs an additional $\log |M_i|$ bits per transcript to specify the boundary — and if the worst-case transcript length is $L$, this is $O(\log L)$ per copy, yielding $O(n \log L)$ overhead.
+If transcript $M_i$ can be up to $L$ bits long, you need approximately $\log L$ bits per copy to specify boundaries — either as explicit delimiters or as prefix-free length encoding. This yields $O(n \log L)$ total overhead. With $L \approx \text{IC}_K(f)$, the overhead is $O(n \log \text{IC}_K(f))$, which for typical $f$ is $O(n \log n)$ in the input size.
 
